# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RestOS — COUNTER TILL (apps/pos-electron). One file per PHYSICAL machine.
#
# Copy to counter.env on the till, fill in from ops/ids.env, and load it from
# ops/startup/restos-counter.bat. Every variable below was derived by grepping process.env and
# the *_ENV constants across apps/pos-electron/src and packages/device-config/src.
#
# ⚠ THE THREE IDS RESOLVE INDEPENDENTLY AND FALL BACK TO A DEV SEED PER KEY. Forget one and the
#   till silently runs under a hardcoded seed id, which no other process has ever heard of. A
#   value that is present but blank or has a stray space REFUSES to start — that refusal is the
#   good case. Set all three, on every device.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ═══ identity — the values nothing anywhere cross-checks ═════════════════════════════════════

# Must equal services/api's BOOTSTRAP_ORG_ID. From ops/ids.env: ORG_ID
# WRONG VALUE: all four processes report success and this till never sees a menu. No error.
RESTOS_ORG_ID=

# Must be IN services/api's ENABLED_BRANCHES. From ops/ids.env: BRANCH_ID
# WRONG VALUE: the menu arrives and every tile reads "no price set" — prices are looked up per
# (branch, channel) with no fallback to a house price (01-F60).
RESTOS_BRANCH_ID=

# Unique per machine. From ops/ids.env: COUNTER_DEVICE_ID
# WRONG VALUE: two devices sharing one id fork one outbox between two processes. There is no
# recovery from this: the ledger is append-only (01-F1) and identity keys the outbox (01-F8).
RESTOS_DEVICE_ID=


# ═══ cloud link ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The FULL WebSocket URL INCLUDING THE PATH. `ws://host:8080` alone does not connect.
# Unset = fully offline: the till still sells, prints and settles (00 §5.1, 01-F17) and shows
# Cloud OFF on the honesty strip. That is a supported state, not a fault.
# WRONG VALUE: Cloud OFF for ever. The till keeps working; nobody is told the menu is stale
# except the strip, which is exactly what it is there for.
RESTOS_CLOUD_URL=ws://CLOUD_HOST:8080/sync

# Minted for THIS EXACT device id by:
#   pnpm -C services/sync-gateway provision-device --org <ORG_ID> --branch <BRANCH_ID> \
#        --device <COUNTER_DEVICE_ID> --class counter_electron
# This is a BOOTSTRAP value only — renewals are persisted by the device from here on, so the
# token in this file goes stale and that is fine. Do not re-provision to "refresh" it.
# WRONG VALUE: rejected at hello, Cloud OFF, no menu, and the till keeps selling offline.
RESTOS_DEVICE_TOKEN=


# ═══ WHERE FOOD TICKETS GO — the one line you must not leave out ═════════════════════════════

# ⚠ THIS BLOCK CHANGED (August 2026). It used to say "THERE IS NO PRINTER TRANSPORT IN THIS
#   PRODUCT" and told you to route everything to a screen. There is one now (03-F1 / 18 §10,
#   RESTOS_PRINTER below), so a branch with a real thermal printer should route to PAPER and set
#   that variable. A branch with NO printer still routes to a screen, for the reasons below.
#
# The default for every station is `paper`. With no RESTOS_PRINTER set, the transport reports
# that the printer did not answer on every attempt, and then every single order does this:
#   send to kitchen -> 3 attempts exhaust (~20 s) -> 03-F5 raises an S1 alarm band with a
#   repeating sound that a cashier must acknowledge BY HAND, ON EVERY ORDER -> a PERMANENT
#   kot.print_failed row is appended to an append-only ledger that can never be cleaned -> the
#   manager is alarmed (05-F3) and vendor support is paged on kot.print_failed rates (15-F14).
# The kitchen never gets the ticket either way. `*=screen` routes every station to the pass
# screen instead: no bytes, no attempt, no band, no ledger event.
#
# ⚠ AND THAT LAST SENTENCE IS TRUE OF KITCHEN TICKETS ONLY. It read as though `*=screen` made a
#   printerless till quiet, and it does not. Station routing is consulted in ONE place —
#   createKotPrinter's `routesToPaper` — and receipts and cash slips are not station-routed at
#   all, because 03-F22's per-station choice has no equivalent for the customer's copy. So with
#   no RESTOS_PRINTER below, on top of a screen-routed kitchen:
#     * EVERY SETTLEMENT enqueues a receipt, burns 3 attempts and raises an S1 band the cashier
#       clears BY HAND, on every sale (03-F5, 03-F12);
#     * the FIRST such failure appends one permanent printer.status_changed(offline) row
#       (03-F54). It is a TRANSITION, so it is one row and not one per sale — and it is NOT a
#       kot.print_failed, which is a KOT fact this path deliberately never writes;
#     * shift close and day summary take 03-F34's column refusal instead (see the next block),
#       and their bands are recorded nowhere: 01 §4 carries no slip.print_failed.
#   Tell the cashier this band is expected and how to clear it, or attach a printer. There is
#   currently no configuration that makes a printerless till silent.
#
# ⚠ SET THIS TO `*=paper` (or leave it out) ONLY IF RESTOS_PRINTER BELOW NAMES A REAL PRINTER.
#   `*=screen` requires ops/env/kitchen.env to be deployed and the pass screen to be RUNNING. A
#   station routed to a screen that nobody is looking at loses its tickets in silence — which is
#   the one failure mode quieter than the band above. This till cannot verify that the branch has
#   a screen (the device registry does not reach a device), so it applies the setting and says
#   "NOT VERIFIED" on its boot line rather than claiming a pass.
# Syntax: comma-separated <station>=paper|screen|both, with `*` setting the default,
#   e.g. *=screen,tandoor=paper for a screen kitchen whose tandoor still prints.
# A malformed entry REFUSES THE WHOLE SETTING and falls back to paper (i.e. to the band above),
# naming the offending entries on the boot line. Read that line after every change.
RESTOS_STATION_ROUTES=*=screen

# ⚠ THE PRINTER ITSELF (03-F1 / 18 §10). Unset = this till has no printer of its own, which is
#   what makes the band above the honest signal rather than a bug. Three forms, one per cable:
#
#   RESTOS_PRINTER=tcp://192.168.1.50:9100   A NETWORK printer (Ethernet or wifi). The port may
#                                            be left off; it defaults to 9100. PREFER THIS FORM:
#                                            it is the only one that can read the paper sensor.
#   RESTOS_PRINTER=windows://KOT             A USB printer ON A WINDOWS TILL. Windows has no
#                                            device node, so the printer must first be INSTALLED
#                                            and SHARED on this machine (Printer properties ->
#                                            Sharing -> Share this printer), and the value is
#                                            that SHARE NAME. Give it a short name with no
#                                            spaces. The share must live on THIS machine — a
#                                            printer shared from another PC is refused.
#   RESTOS_PRINTER=device:///dev/usb/lp0     A USB printer on a LINUX till. The path must be
#                                            absolute, and the account running RestOS must be in
#                                            the `lp` group or every ticket fails with EACCES.
#
# ⚠ THE TWO USB FORMS ARE WRITE-ONLY AND CANNOT SEE PAPER-OUT. They take the bytes and say
#   nothing back, so a roll that runs out reads as a PRINTED TICKET and the chit is lost with no
#   band (03-F40 names exactly this failure). Only tcp:// asks the sensor after every document
#   and holds the job until the roll is replaced (03-F41). If the printer has an Ethernet port,
#   use it.
# ⚠ A VALUE THIS TILL CANNOT READ IS REFUSED WHOLE and the till starts with no printer at all —
#   never a guess. The boot line names what was rejected and why. Read it after every change.
# ⚠ NO PRINTER HAS EVER BEEN TESTED AGAINST THIS CODE (K-8). It has been exercised against a
#   loopback socket and a file. Expect to spend time on the first real one.
# RESTOS_PRINTER=

# The printer model, which decides the column width a ticket is laid out against. It is the
# MODEL, not the cable — the cable is RESTOS_PRINTER above.
# Unset resolves to a conservative 32-column record, and a KOT needs 42 — so every ticket is
# refused before a byte is sent, with an S1 band naming both column counts. SET THIS THE MOMENT
# YOU SET RESTOS_PRINTER: a link with no model prints nothing at all.
#
# ⚠ IT IS NOT IRRELEVANT UNDER `*=screen`, AND THIS LINE SAID IT WAS ("because no print job is
#   ever created"). Station routing suppresses KOTs and nothing else, so the other three document
#   types are still rendered against whatever this resolves to, and 03-F49's floors differ per
#   document: KOT 42, shift-close slip 35, day summary 34, receipt 32. At the unset default of 32
#   the receipt renders (32 >= 32) and then fails at TRANSMIT if no printer is attached, while
#   SHIFT CLOSE AND DAY SUMMARY ARE REFUSED AT RENDER (03-F34) with an S1 band naming the column
#   counts — every night, on a till whose kitchen is a screen. Setting a real model here is what
#   makes those two documents renderable; attaching a printer is what makes any of them print.
# RESTOS_KOT_PRINTER=TH230

# Diagnostic only: a directory into which every transmitted document is written as a PDF, one
# page per cut. It renders what OUR OWN encoder thinks the bytes mean, so it is not evidence
# about any real printer, and paper never runs out in it. MUST NOT be set in service — it
# replaces the transport whose alarm is the only honest signal that no printer is attached.
# RESTOS_PRINT_TO_FILE=


# ═══ staff — read this before go-live ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ⚠ THIS IS A DEV SEED BEING USED IN PRODUCTION, KNOWINGLY, BECAUSE THERE IS NO OTHER OPTION.
# Device admission (01-F47) admits DEVICES, not PEOPLE, and no transport exists that can deliver
# a real staff roster to a till. These three keys seed three users — you cannot add, rename or
# remove them, and the names below are the names on the tiles.
#
# ⚠ CHANGED August 2026 — ONE PIN FOR THE WHOLE ROSTER WAS AN AUTHORIZATION HOLE.
# `RESTOS_DEV_PIN` used to seed all three. That meant the branch manager's row opened with the
# digits both cashiers type 20-60 times a shift, so every manager-only control was one tile-tap
# away (02-F22 day open / float; 02-F20 large paid-out approval), and 02-F38's "you cannot
# approve your own request" refused nothing, because one secret opened two user ids.
# There is ONE KEY PER PERSON now and NO FALLBACK between them.
#
# GIVE EACH PERSON DIFFERENT DIGITS. Two people sharing digits here recreates the hole by hand:
# the till then writes the wrong name into a ledger that cannot be corrected in place (01-F1,
# 02-F41), and the manager's authority is held by whoever knows her number.
#
# A key left blank = that person is NOT on the identification grid and cannot sign in. That is
# deliberate (an absent tile is visible; a shared credential is not) and the boot line names
# everybody it could not seed. In particular: leave RESTOS_DEV_PIN_HINA blank and THE DAY CANNOT
# BE OPENED, so no shift opens and no sale is recorded (02-F22).
#
# All three blank = the grid is EMPTY and nobody can sign in. The till is unusable.
# 4+ digits each. PINs live only in this file, never in source; rotate by editing here.
#
# NOTE the first key keeps its old name on purpose — ops/startup/restos-counter.bat refuses to
# start without RESTOS_DEV_PIN, so renaming it would stop the till booting. It is now ONLY
# Ayesha's PIN and can no longer open the manager's row.
RESTOS_DEV_PIN=
RESTOS_DEV_PIN_BILAL=
RESTOS_DEV_PIN_HINA=

# ⚠ NEVER SET THIS IN A REAL RESTAURANT. It seeds a fake demo menu (three categories, eight
#   items) into a till at catalog version 0. It refuses to run once a real menu has arrived, so
#   it cannot overwrite one — but a till that has not yet synced would sell fictional food at
#   fictional prices, and 01-F53 freezes the price into the ledger permanently.
# RESTOS_DEV_MENU=1


# ═══ screen ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Pixels per inch of the actual glass. Measured from the OS where the OS reports a physical
# size; on Windows that is usually available, and where it is not the app ASSUMES a 15.6" panel
# and says "assumed" on its boot line.
# ⚠ BEING WRONG HERE LOOKS EXACTLY LIKE BEING RIGHT. Every touch target is sized in millimetres
#   of glass and rendered through this number, so a wrong density renders a 20 mm key at 9 mm
#   with nothing on screen looking broken. Read the boot line; if it says "assumed", measure the
#   panel's width in mm and set this. Accepted range 50..800; outside it is ignored.
# RESTOS_PANEL_PPI=

# ═══ the rest — safe to leave unset ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Aging thresholds in minutes, <order_type>=<amber>/<red>, comma-separated. Defaults are
# dine_in/takeaway/pickup 10/20 and delivery 15/25. ONE org policy: the counter, the pass screen
# and the manager console must all read the same value or three surfaces disagree about whether
# the food is late. A malformed entry is refused WHOLE and the defaults stay in force.
# RESTOS_AGING_THRESHOLDS=dine_in=10/20,delivery=15/25

# Who owns the signal that food reached the customer: settlement | pass | counter | waiter.
# Default `settlement` — the till marks lines served when the bill is settled, which is right for
# a branch with no waiter. ⚠ MUST BE THE SAME VALUE ON THE TILL AND THE PASS SCREEN. It is NOT
# the same list as the pass screen's RESTOS_READY_SIGNAL_OWNER; do not copy one into the other.
# RESTOS_SERVE_SIGNAL_OWNER=settlement

# Kitchen quick-tags offered when adding a note to a line. Unset = no quick-tag control at all.
# Comma-separated free text. Nothing caps the count today, and enough of them will push a
# primary control off the screen.
# RESTOS_QUICK_TAGS=No chilli,Extra spicy,No onion

# T1 | T2 | T3. Unset = T1 is ASSUMED and reported as assumed. Leave it unset unless you know
# what it changes: it gates whether a printed ticket advances its lines, and with screen routing
# nothing is printed anyway.
# RESTOS_HARDWARE_TIER=

# LAN mesh (01-F12 manual-IP directory). ⚠ NOTHING HOSTS THE MESH TODAY, so setting these
# changes what the boot line says and nothing else. The till and the pass screen exchange orders
# via the CLOUD, which means a WAN outage isolates them from each other. Leave unset.
# RESTOS_LAN_PORT=7311
# RESTOS_LAN_HOST=0.0.0.0
# RESTOS_LAN_PEERS=<device_id>@192.168.1.20:7311

# Set by electron-vite in development only. Never set it on a real till: it points the renderer
# at a dev server that will not be running.
# ELECTRON_RENDERER_URL=
